><span class="enc-snip">Psamtik III was the last pharaoh of Egypt's Twenty-sixth Dynasty, reigning briefly in the sixth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/psaumis-of-camarina"><span class="enc-name">Psaumis of Camarina</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Psaumis was a citizen of Camarina, a Greek city on the south coast of Sicily, who won an Olympic victory with a chariot, probably in 452 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/psekups"><span class="enc-name">Psekups</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Psekups is a river of the North-West Caucasus in Russia, a left tributary of the Kuban, flowing north from the Caucasus foothills through Krasnodar Krai and Adygea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pshavi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pshavi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pshavi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pshavi is a historic mountain region of north-eastern Georgia, in the valley of the Pshavi Aragvi river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pshavs"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pshavs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pshavs</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pshavs are a Georgian highland people of Pshavi, in the upper valleys of the Aragvi and Iori rivers in north-eastern Georgia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pshemakho-kotsev"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pshemakho-kotsev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pshemakho Kotsev</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pshemakho Kotsev was a Kabardian politician who led the government of the Mountainous Republic of the Northern Caucasus in 1918–1919.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pshish"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pshish.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pshish</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pshish is a river of the North-West Caucasus in Russia, a left tributary of the Kuban.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pskov"><span class="enc-name">Pskov</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pskov is one of Russia's oldest cities, on the Velikaya river near the Estonian border, first recorded in the chronicles under 903.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pskov-krom"><span class="enc-name">Pskov Krom</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pskov Krom is the medieval citadel of Pskov in north-western Russia, standing on a rocky promontory where the Pskova River flows into the Velikaya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/psusennes-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Psusennes I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Psusennes I was a king of the Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt who ruled from Tanis in the late 11th century BCE for about 46 years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/psusennes-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Psusennes II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Psusennes II was the last king of the Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt, reigning in the mid-10th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/psychoanalysis"><span class="enc-name">Psychoanalysis</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Psychoanalysis is a theory and method of treating mental disorders through the investigation of unconscious processes, developed by Sigmund Freud.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/psychological-warfare"><span class="enc-name">Psychological warfare</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Psychological warfare is the use of fear, deception and propaganda to weaken an enemy's will to resist.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/psylli"><span class="enc-name">Psylli</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Psylli were an ancient Libyan people living on the shores of the Gulf of Sirte in present-day Libya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptoion"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptoion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptoion</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ptoion was a sanctuary of Apollo Ptoios on Mount Ptoon in Boeotia, near the city of Acraephia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaeus-of-commagene"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemaeus of Commagene</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemaeus was the founder of the kingdom of Commagene in south-eastern Anatolia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemaic Kingdom</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ptolemaic Kingdom was a Hellenistic state in Egypt founded in 305 BCE by Ptolemy I Soter, one of Alexander the Great's generals, after the break-up of the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-apion"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-apion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy Apion</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy Apion was a son of Ptolemy VIII who became king of Cyrene when Cyrenaica was separated from Egypt around 116 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy I Soter</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy I Soter was a Macedonian general of Alexander the Great who became ruler of Egypt after Alexander's death.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy II Philadelphus was king of Egypt from 283 to 246 BCE, born on Kos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iii-euergetes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iii-euergetes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy III Euergetes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy III Euergetes was king of Ptolemaic Egypt from 246 to 222 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy IV Philopator</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy IV Philopator was king of Ptolemaic Egypt from 221 to 204 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy Keraunos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy Keraunos was a son of Ptolemy I Soter who was passed over for the Egyptian succession.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-of-mauretania"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-of-mauretania.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy of Mauretania</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy of Mauretania was the last king of Mauretania, son of Juba II and Cleopatra Selene and a grandson of Mark Antony and Cleopatra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-son-of-mennaeus"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy son of Mennaeus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy son of Mennaeus was the ruler of the Ituraeans in the 1st century BCE, with his capital at Chalcis in the Beqaa valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-vi-philometor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-vi-philometor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy VI Philometor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy VI Philometor was king of Ptolemaic Egypt from 180 to 145 BCE, at times jointly with his brother Ptolemy VIII and his sister-wife Cleopatra II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-viii-physcon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-viii-physcon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy VIII Physcon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy VIII, nicknamed Physcon, was a Ptolemaic king who quarrelled for decades with his brother Ptolemy VI.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ptolemy XII Auletes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ptolemy XII Auletes was king of Ptolemaic Egypt from 80 to 58 BCE and again from 55 to 51 BCE, and father of Cleopatra VII.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pua-district"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pua-district.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pua district</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pua is a district in the upper Nan valley of Nan Province in northern Thailand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/puabi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/puabi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Puabi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Puabi was a queen or noblewoman of Ur around 2600 BCE, known from her richly furnished tomb in the Royal Cemetery, excavated by Leonard Woolley in 1927–1928.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/publius-canidius-crassus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/publius-canidius-crassus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Publius Canidius Crassus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Publius Canidius Crassus was a Roman general and partisan of Mark Antony, suffect consul in 40 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/publius-carisius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/publius-carisius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Publius Carisius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Publius Carisius was a Roman general under Augustus, legate in